-### Sensitivity-Aware Vector Clustering
-Groups similar Gaussian attributes into a codebook to reduce storage by storing indices instead of full vectors. Choose when memory bandwidth is the primary constraint and attribute similarity is high [12167-sensitivity-aware-vector-clustering.md].
-
-### Compact 3D Gaussian Representation (C3DGS)
-Uses a compact representation of Gaussians to reduce memory footprint. Choose when rendering quality must be maintained with significantly lower memory usage [12927-compact-3d-gaussian-representation-c3dgs.md].
-
-### Monte Carlo Specular Energy Aggregator
-Aggregates specular energy using Monte Carlo methods. Choose when handling specular highlights efficiently in differentiable rendering [15439-monte-carlo-specular-energy-aggregator.md].
+**Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
+*   **Matrix Traversal:** Compressing large color matrices is slow due to traversal time. Using `torch::where` and `torch::index_select` improves speed but is not fast enough for full replacement [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Line Breaks:** Using line break characters adds an extra operator in writing and decoding, increasing overhead [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Formula Ambiguity:** The decoding formula $f = I \times 10^{-d}$ is mathematically inconsistent with standard decimal representation if $d$ is the number of decimal digits or the digit value. Implementers should verify if $d$ represents a shift or if the formula is a typo for $f = I + \text{fraction}$ [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
--   **Time Complexity:** The compression/decompression is $O(1)$ per value, but matrix traversal overhead can be significant [S1, unofficial].
--   **Memory Complexity:** Reduces data size by encoding values as ASCII strings, but the overhead of string manipulation may negate benefits for small datasets [S1, unofficial].
--   **Performance:** Gains approximately 6% or less improvement in training speed [S1, unofficial].
--   **Strengths:** Simple implementation; reduces data size for color matrices with low precision [S1, unofficial].
--   **Limitations:** Slow matrix traversal; not fast enough for full replacement of binary serialization; modest speed gains [S1, unofficial].
--   **When to Use:** When training speed is bottlenecked by data transmission and attributes can be quantized to low precision [S1, unofficial].
--   **When Not to Use:** When high precision is required or when matrix traversal overhead dominates [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Training Speed Improvement:** Gains approximately 6% or less improvement in training speed [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Storage/Transmission:** Reduces data size significantly for color matrices by limiting to 21 alternatives per item [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Not suitable for full color matrix compression replacement due to traversal overhead [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Complexity:** $O(N)$ for encoding/decoding $N$ values, but with high constant factors for matrix operations [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-The method was introduced in "I3DGS: Improve 3D Gaussian Splatting from Multiple Dimensions" by F. Author et al. in 2024 [S1, unofficial]. It is part of an effort to improve the training speed of 3D Gaussian Splatting models by optimizing data transmission, learning, structure design, and compression [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Origin:** Proposed in "I3DGS: Improve 3D Gaussian Splatting from Multiple Dimensions" [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Context:** Part of efforts to improve training speed of 3D Gaussian Splatting models through compression and optimization [S1, unofficial].
 
